{
  "concept": "A Fibonacci heap is a circular doubly linked list of min-heap-ordered trees whose abstract tree picture and concrete four-pointer node structure are two views of the same data, and whose root list makes insertion an O(1) splice.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel label '(a)' at left of upper view",
    "Panel label '(b)' at left of lower view",
    "H.min label in italic serif with downward arrow pointing at root node 3, drawn above each panel",
    "Root list nodes (light gray filled circles, black outline, black numerals): 23, 7, 3, 17, 24",
    "Panel (a) dashed horizontal line connecting the five root nodes in order 23–7–3–17–24 (circular root list, drawn abstractly)",
    "Panel (a) solid tree edges: 3→18, 3→52, 3→38; 18→39; 38→41; 17→30; 24→26, 24→46; 26→35",
    "Child nodes (light gray): 52, 38, 41, 30, 46, 35",
    "Marked nodes drawn as solid black circles with bold white numerals: 18, 39, 26",
    "Panel (b) root list rendered as a circular doubly linked list: an upper rounded 'wrap-around' rail carrying rightward (next/right) arrows between 23→7→3→17→24 and back to 23, and a lower rounded rail carrying leftward (prev/left) arrows",
    "Panel (b) sibling lists for each child group (18-52-38), (30), (26-46), (39), (41), (35), each drawn as its own circular doubly linked list with upper right-pointing rail and lower left-pointing rail, including self-loop rails for single-child lists",
    "Panel (b) vertical double arrows between parent and child: downward arrow = parent's child pointer, upward arrow = child's parent pointer (3↔18, 3↔52, 3↔38 group, 17↔30, 24↔26, 24↔46, 18↔39, 38↔41, 26↔35)",
    "Legend/caption strip explaining: gray = unmarked, black = marked (has lost one child since last becoming a root)",
    "Optional inserted node (used by the insert demo) shown in a highlight color, spliced between 24 and 23 in the root list",
    "Pointer-field callout labels used on click: parent, child, left, right"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the tidy tree drawing and the tangle of bidirectional pointers describe exactly the same heap — tree edges are shorthand for parent/child pointers, and each horizontal sibling row is really a circular doubly linked list.",
      "label": "Representation",
      "options": ["(a) abstract tree view", "(b) pointer diagram", "side by side"],
      "default": "(a) abstract tree view"
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectNode",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That every node stores exactly four links — parent, one arbitrary child, left sibling, right sibling — and that sibling links close into a ring, so a node has no notion of 'first' or 'last' child."
    },
    {
      "id": "insertPhase",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "That FIB-HEAP-INSERT does no tree work at all: it makes a one-node tree, splices it into the circular root list with a constant number of pointer writes, and only compares its key against H.min.",
      "label": "FIB-HEAP-INSERT(H, 21)",
      "states": ["idle", "new node", "splice into root list", "compare with H.min", "done"],
      "default": "idle"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The abstract view",
      "narration": "Five min-heap-ordered trees hang off a single root list, and H.min points at the smallest root, 3. Black nodes — 18, 39, 26 — are marked: each has already lost one child since it last became a root.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(a) abstract tree view", "insertPhase": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The same heap, in pointers",
      "narration": "Switch to the implementation view: the dashed root line becomes a circular doubly linked list, and each row of siblings gets its own ring. The heap's shape never changed — only how much of the bookkeeping we draw.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer diagram", "insertPhase": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Four pointers per node",
      "narration": "Click node 18, then 52, then 30. Each lights up the same four fields: parent, child, left, right. Single children like 30 point left and right to themselves — a ring of one.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer diagram", "insertPhase": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insert: make a lone tree",
      "narration": "Inserting key 21 creates a brand-new single-node tree with degree 0, mark = FALSE, and left and right pointing to itself.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer diagram", "insertPhase": "new node" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insert: splice into the root list",
      "narration": "Four pointer writes stitch 21 between 24 and 23 in the circular root list. No comparisons among roots, no restructuring — this is the whole of the work.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer diagram", "insertPhase": "splice into root list" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insert: one comparison",
      "narration": "21 is compared only against H.min's key, 3. Since 21 > 3, H.min stays put and the insertion finishes in O(1).",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer diagram", "insertPhase": "compare with H.min" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Back to the abstract picture",
      "narration": "Return to view (a): the heap is now six trees in the root list. Lazy insertion leaves the mess for a later EXTRACT-MIN to consolidate.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(a) abstract tree view", "insertPhase": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The root list is just a circular doubly linked list, so a Fibonacci heap can add a tree with a constant number of pointer writes — it defers all tree-fixing work, which is exactly why insert is O(1).",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing the same node positions, labeled (a) top and (b) bottom at the far left in serif type. Nodes are equal-sized circles with black outlines: unmarked nodes light gray with black numerals, marked nodes (18, 39, 26) solid black with bold white numerals. Root row across the top of each panel, left to right: 23, 7, 3, 17, 24, with 3 roughly centered and an italic 'H.min' label above it joined by a short downward arrow. Panel (a): roots joined by a thin horizontal dashed line; children hang below on thin solid straight edges — 3 has three children 18, 52, 38 fanned below it, 18 has child 39, 38 has child 41, 17 has child 30, 24 has children 26 and 46, 26 has child 35. Panel (b): identical node placement, but each sibling group is wrapped in a pair of long rounded-rectangle rails — an upper rail carrying rightward arrowheads (right/next pointers) and a lower rail carrying leftward arrowheads (left/prev pointers) — so every sibling list, including one-element lists like 30, 39, 41, 35, reads as a closed ring. Between each parent and one child, draw a pair of parallel vertical arrows: down = child pointer, up = parent pointer; only one child per group receives the parent's child arrow, while all children in the group send parent arrows up. Use a saturated accent color (e.g. orange) for click-highlighted pointer fields with small labels 'parent', 'child', 'left', 'right', and a second accent (e.g. teal) for the newly inserted node 21 and the four pointers rewritten during the splice. Keep line weights thin and typography serif to match a textbook figure."
}

